  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-hh-module-category-and-tensor-action": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $k$ be a field and let $H$ be a $k$-vector space with associative unital multiplication $m$, unit $1_H$, coassociative comultiplication $Delta:H\\to H\\otimes_k H$, and counit $\\varepsilon:H\\to k$. Assume $\\Delta$ and $\\varepsilon$ are unital algebra maps; this is the bialgebra interface used on this page. A left $H$-module is a unital left module in the sense of [[def-left-and-right-modules]]: its action satisfies $1_Hv=v$ and $(hh')v=h(h'v)$. Let $H\\text{-}\\mathrm{Mod}$ be the category whose objects are all left $H$-modules and whose morphisms $f:V\\to W$ are $k$-linear maps satisfying $f(hv)=h f(v)$ for every $h\\in H$ and $v\\in V$.\n\nFor left $H$-modules $V,W$, give the algebraic tensor product $V\\otimes_k W$ the candidate action\n\n$$h\\cdot(v\\otimes w)=\\sum h_{(1)}v\\otimes h_{(2)}w,\\qquad \\Delta(h)=\\sum h_{(1)}\\otimes h_{(2)}.$$\n\nThe sums are finite algebraic tensor sums; the tensor product and scalar conventions are those of [[def-hh-scalar-and-tensor-conventions]]. The candidate unit object is $k_\\varepsilon=k$ with $h\\cdot c=\\varepsilon(h)c$. For morphisms $f:V\\to V'$ and $g:W\\to W'$, the candidate tensor morphism is the usual linear map $f\\otimes g$ determined by $(f\\otimes g)(v\\otimes w)=f(v)\\otimes g(w)$ through [[thm-universal-property-of-module-tensor-products]].\n\nThese formulas specify the proposed tensor operations; [[thm-hh-bialgebra-module-tensor-coherence]] proves that they are module actions and that the canonical associator and unit maps are morphisms in $H\\text{-}\\mathrm{Mod}$."
    ],
    "thm-hh-bialgebra-module-tensor-coherence": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field, $H$ a unital associative $k$-algebra, and $\\Delta:H\\to H\\otimes_k H$ and $\\varepsilon:H\\to k$ linear maps. Suppose first that $H$ is a bialgebra: $\\Delta$ is coassociative and a unital algebra map, and $\\varepsilon$ is a counit and a unital algebra map. For the category $H\\text{-}\\mathrm{Mod}$ and candidate tensor action of [[def-hh-module-category-and-tensor-action]], the tensor product of two left $H$-modules is a left $H$-module, $k_\\varepsilon$ is the unit module, tensor products of $H$-linear maps are $H$-linear, and the canonical associator and unit maps are $H$-linear. They are natural, and the pentagon and triangle diagrams commute. Thus these operations define a monoidal category of left $H$-modules; no symmetry or braiding is asserted.\n\nConversely, start with only the unital associative algebra $H$ and the linear maps $\\Delta,\\varepsilon$. If the displayed formula of [[def-hh-module-category-and-tensor-action]] makes $V\\otimes_k W$ a left $H$-module for every pair of left $H$-modules, $k_\\varepsilon$ is a left $H$-module, and the canonical associator and both canonical unit maps are $H$-linear, then $\\Delta$ is a coassociative counital unital algebra map and $\\varepsilon$ is a counital unital algebra map. Hence these data make $H$ a bialgebra."
    ],
    "def-hh-left-dual-of-a-finite-hopf-module": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $H$ be a bialgebra over a field $k$, let $S:H\\to H$ be an antipode satisfying\n\n$$\\sum S(h_{(1)})h_{(2)}=\\varepsilon(h)1_H=\\sum h_{(1)}S(h_{(2)}),$$\n\nand let $V$ be a finite-dimensional left $H$-module. The algebraic dual $V^*$ is the vector space of linear functionals $V\\to k$ from [[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]. Give it the candidate left $H$-action\n\n$$ (h\\cdot f)(v):=f(S(h)\\cdot v). $$\n\nSet $\\operatorname{ev}:V^*\\otimes V\\to k$, $f\\otimes v\\mapsto f(v)$, and set $\\operatorname{coev}:k\\to V\\otimes V^*$, $1\\mapsto\\sum_i e_i\\otimes e^i$ for any finite basis $(e_i)$ and its dual basis $(e^i)$. The tensor in this formula is basis-independent by [[lem-hh-finite-tensor-duality-and-canonical-coevaluation]]. In this page's convention, “left dual” means these displayed orientations $V^*\\otimes V\\to k$ and $k\\to V\\otimes V^*$. Some sources use “right dual” for the same orientations; the formulas here fix the convention. The next theorem proves the candidate action and maps give a left dual, without assuming $S$ bijective."
    ],
    "thm-hh-left-dual-evaluation-coevaluation-and-triangle-identities": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H$ be a bialgebra over a field $k$ with antipode $S$, meaning a linear map satisfying\n\n$$\\sum S(h_{(1)})h_{(2)}=\\varepsilon(h)1_H=\\sum h_{(1)}S(h_{(2)}).$$\n\nLet $V$ be a finite-dimensional left $H$-module and give $V^*$ the candidate action and maps of [[def-hh-left-dual-of-a-finite-hopf-module]]. Then $S(ab)=S(b)S(a)$ and $S(1_H)=1_H$; the candidate action makes $V^*$ a left $H$-module; evaluation $V^*\\otimes V\\to k$ and coevaluation $k\\to V\\otimes V^*$ are $H$-linear; and the two triangle composites are identities. No bijectivity of $S$ is assumed."
    ],
    "lem-hh-right-dual-and-bidual-conditions": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H$ be a Hopf algebra over a field $k$ with antipode $S$, and assume for the right-dual construction that $S$ is bijective. Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional left $H$-module and let $V^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_k(V,k)$ be its algebraic dual. In this page's convention, define the right-dual action on $V^*$ by\n\n$$ (h\\cdot f)(v):=f(S^{-1}(h)\\cdot v), $$\n\nwith evaluation $\\widetilde{\\operatorname{ev}}:V\\otimes V^*\\to k$, $v\\otimes f\\mapsto f(v)$, and coevaluation $\\widetilde{\\operatorname{coev}}:k\\to V^*\\otimes V$, $1\\mapsto\\sum_i e^i\\otimes e_i$. These maps are $H$-linear and satisfy the two right-dual triangle identities. The bijectivity hypothesis is used only for this $S^{-1}$ construction; the left-dual construction earlier on the page needs no such hypothesis.\n\nLet $j_V:V\\to V^{**}$ be the ordinary vector-space bidual map, $j_V(v)(f)=f(v)$, where $V^{**}$ has the twice-applied left-dual action. Then\n\n$$ h\\cdot j_V(v)=j_V(S^2(h)\\cdot v). $$\n\nFor a fixed finite-dimensional module $V$, $j_V$ is $H$-linear exactly when $h$ and $S^2(h)$ act identically on $V$ for every $h$. Thus $S^2=\\mathrm{id}_H$ is sufficient for $j_V$ to be $H$-linear on every finite-dimensional module. If $H$ itself is finite-dimensional, the converse follows by taking its regular module. If an invertible group-like element $p$ satisfies $S^2(h)=php^{-1}$, then $j_{p,V}(v):=j_V(p\\cdot v)$ is a natural monoidal $H$-module isomorphism $V\\to V^{**}$ on finite-dimensional modules."
    ],
